/**
 * OQCA v1.7 — the owner directive of 2026-09-11, tested where it can be.
 *
 * THE HARD RULE IS THE ONE THIS FILE EXISTS FOR: _"Do not make ONIQ appear
 * autonomous through scripted demonstrations. The test must be able to prove
 * that the decision was generated by the runtime from observed state, rather
 * than being preselected by the test. The system must be able to surprise the
 * test within defined acceptance constraints."_
 *
 * A test that fed one world and asserted one goal id would satisfy every
 * functional requirement and prove nothing — the id would be a fact about the
 * fixture. So the §5 block below never names an expected winner. It asserts
 * INVARIANTS that hold whatever ONIQ decides (the winner is the argmax of the
 * published score; the winner's subject is one the observer actually reported;
 * two different worlds give two different answers) and then drives sixty
 * pseudo-random worlds whose winners nobody here knows.
 */
